%S A402275 111,72,0,70,10,136,30,2,78,30,100,10,30,10,10,46,30,60,42,50,24,10,
%T A402275 30,8,20,10,36,28,0,8,40,40,12,16,38,12,10,8,0,28,12,2,2,66,16,18,2,
%U A402275 32,0,18,14,30,2,24,28,12,10,18,0,36,0,2,12,0,106,10,14,0
%N A402275 Span of the n-th maximal cluster of primes with consecutive gaps at most 10.
%C A402275 The initial span a(1)=111 is first exceeded by the cluster 331 to 467, giving a(6)=136. This is the only exceedance found through 10^11, although the Hardy-Littlewood prime-tuples conjecture predicts infinitely many.
%C A402275 For jump limits 2, 4 and 6, the initial spans 5, 21 and 87 are provably unique maxima since every later cluster has span at most 2, 16 and 78, respectively (A399292).
%C A402275 The cluster 331 to 467 contains 25 primes, fewer than the initial count 30 (A402276).
%H A402275 Jwalin Bhatt, <a href="/A402275/b402275.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%e A402275 The first cluster is {2, ..., 113} and has span 113-2 = 111, so a(1) = 111.
%e A402275 The second cluster is {127, ..., 199}, so a(2) = 72.
%e A402275 The third cluster is {211}, so a(3) = 0.
%e A402275 The sixth cluster is {331, ..., 467}, so a(6) = 136.
%t A402275 s={}; p1=2; Do[p=p1; While[NextPrime[p]-p<=10, p=NextPrime[p]]; AppendTo[s, p-p1]; p1=NextPrime[p], {i, 68}]; s
%o A402275 (Python)
%o A402275 from sympy import nextprime
%o A402275 from itertools import islice
%o A402275 def gen_A402275():
%o A402275     start = p = 2
%o A402275     while True:
%o A402275         q = int(nextprime(p))
%o A402275         if q - p > 10:
%o A402275             yield p - start
%o A402275             start = q
%o A402275         p = q
%o A402275 print(list(islice(gen_A402275(), 68)))
%Y A402275 Cf. A399292, A400690, A401842, A401896, A402276.
%K A402275 nonn,new
%O A402275 1,1
